{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 17, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Crusader nobleman and one of the most prominent figures in the County of Tripoli during the 12th century. He was born around the year 1140 and his family had deep roots in the region which included his grandfather Raymond II who founded the County of Tripoli. Raymond III became the count in 1152 when he was just a young boy following the death of his father Raymond II.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\nRaymond III's reign was marked by a series of military and political challenges. Early in his rule he faced pressure from Muslim forces led by Nur ad-Din who sought to expand his influence over the Crusader states. In 1164 Raymond allied with the Assassins of the Nizari sect to fight against Nur ad-Din. This alliance provided critical support but it also highlighted the complex political dynamics of the region where different factions often collaborated or clashed for control.\n\nOne of the defining events of Raymond III's rule was the Siege of Harim in 1164. Raymond along with Baldwin III of Jerusalem and other Crusader leaders attempted to capture the fortress of Harim which was held by Nur ad-Din. Despite the Crusaders' efforts they were ultimately forced to retreat due to the strong defenses and the arrival of reinforcements for Nur ad-Din. This failure demonstrated the growing power of the Zengid dynasty and the challenges that the Crusader states faced in maintaining their territories.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\nIn the following years Raymond III continued to be a key player in the politics of Outremer. He was involved in the Third Crusade which began in 1187 after the fall of Jerusalem to Saladin. Raymond played a crucial role in the negotiations and diplomacy that followed the loss of Jerusalem. He was among the few Crusader leaders who remained in the region during the years leading up to the arrival of major Crusader armies and he worked to maintain some level of stability and resistance against Muslim forces.\n\nRaymond III also focused on strengthening the defenses of his county. He invested in fortifications and alliances to protect Tripoli from external threats. His efforts included the construction and maintenance of castles which were vital in defending against attacks. Additionally Raymond sought to improve relations with other Christian factions including the Knights Templar and the Knights Hospitaller who were significant military and financial powers in the region.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\nRaymond III's later years were characterized by a mix of military engagements and diplomatic efforts. He participated in the relief of the Siege of Acre in 1189–1191 which was a significant Crusader victory during the Third Crusade. His presence and leadership were important in the successful defense of the city against Saladin's forces.\n\nIn 1187 Raymond married Eschiva of Bures who was the widow of Bohemond III of Antioch. This marriage was a strategic move aimed at securing alliances and strengthening his political position within the Crusader states. Eschiva brought with her a dowry and connections that helped Raymond in his efforts to stabilize and expand his county.\n\nRaymond III died in July 1187 at the siege of Hattin before the decisive battle that led to the fall of Jerusalem. Although he did not live to see the full impact of the Third Crusade his leadership and contributions were vital in the defense and governance of the County of Tripoli. His legacy endures as one of the most capable and resourceful rulers of the Crusader states during a period of significant turmoil and challenge.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.06853077560663223}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.00012003655137959868}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.006313735153526068}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.29494979977607727}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0